ying a small **single card** like ♣4 or ♠5 risks giving peasants control early.\n- A **pair sequence** like ♥4♣4♠5♦5♠6♦6 or longer might burn through cards quickly but commits many mid-range cards.\n- A **triple with single/attached pair** allows flexibility and preserves follow-up plays.\n- The **triple of 2s (♥2♣2♦2)** is effectively a bomb (three 2s plus Red Joker makes four-of-a-kind if used as a bomb via game logic? No—only four same-rank cards count as bomb; here, we have only three 2s and joker isn\'t rank-based). So it\'s just a triple with possible attachments.\nBut wait: **triple_with_single: ♥2 ♣2 ♦2 RedJoker** is listed — this is valid because RedJoker is being used as an attached card (though unusual, per rules it’s allowed as long as combination fits type). However, using RedJoker now may be wasteful unless necessary.\n\nBest opportunity: Play **triple: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4** — my only true triple, starting low to avoid wasting high cards. This forces opponents to respond with higher triples (which are rare) or pass. Alternatively, **triple_with_pair: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♠5 ♦5** clears more cards and builds momentum.\n\nAlso consider: **pair_straight: ♥4♣4♠5♦5♠6♦6** — six cards down immediately, very efficient. But uses up good pairing material.\n\nHowever, leading with a **triple_with_pair** on 4s anchors the play around a strong, low base and removes seven cards at once. Since peasants likely hold scattered cards, forcing them to beat a complex combo early increases chance they must pass.\n\nReason: Leading with **triple_with_pair: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♠5 ♦5** efficiently clears multiple cards while requiring opponents to beat a relatively high-complexity combination (a triplet with a pair). There are few such combinations in the deck, especially at low ranks. This move pressures both peasants early and exploits my positional advantage as landlord. It avoids burning the Red Joker unnecessarily and keeps options open for future bombs or finishes.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠4", "♥4", "♣4",