ich is allowed.\n\nBut comparing efficiency:\n- **triple_with_pair: ♠A ♣A ♥2 ♣2 ♦2** uses both Aces and all 2s — clears multiple high cards and reduces hand significantly.\n- **triple_with_single: ... + RedJoker** uses our only joker early, which may be risky unless necessary.\n\nBetter strategy: Save the RedJoker for when we need to win a trick uncontested later. Instead, use the strong **triplet 2s with Ace pair** now.\n\nWait — checking validity:  \nThe move **triple_with_pair: ♠A ♣A ♥2 ♣2 ♦2** means triplet of 2s + pair of Aces? No! That violates structure: **triple_with_pair** requires a **triplet of one rank** and **a pair of another** — yes, so **♥2 ♣2 ♦2 (triplet)** + **♠A ♣A (pair)** is valid.\n\nYes — this is a legal **triple_with_pair**.\n\nThis move burns:\n- All three 2s (very high-ranking triplet),\n- Both Aces (reducing burden of high singles),\n- And uses 5 cards in one go.\n\nAfter this, I\'ll be left with: ♠6 ♦6 ♠7 ♣7 ♦8 ♣10 ♥Q ♦Q ♣K RedJoker — 9 cards.\n\nAlternatively, if I play **triple_with_single: ♥2 ♣2 ♦2 RedJoker**, I use four cards but keep both Aces and lose the chance to offload more mid-high cards.\n\nMoreover, playing **RedJoker** now wastes its power — it could win any future round if someone plays a bomb or rocket threat.\n\nThus, best choice: **triple_with_pair: ♥2 ♣2 ♦2 ♠A ♣A**\n\nReason:  \nThis move maximizes card reduction (5 cards played), clears the highest possible triplet (2s) and a strong pair (Aces), avoids wasting the RedJoker, and maintains pressure on opponents. It progresses toward emptying the hand rapidly while preserving key trump cards like RedJoker for future control.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥2", "♣2", "♦2", "♠A", "♣A"]}'}
